./xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:6536:3. **“The full JC2 program is finite at every level” confuses pointwise finiteness with termination.** Finiteness for each fixed \((m,td)\) gives infinitely many books; only \(td\le14\) was enumerated and the degree farm stops at 150. Moreover, killing residue-A would still leave four single-pole \(td=6\) classes, while the composite single-pole residual grows to 212 classes at \(td=16\). There is no uniform \(td\)-bound, induction, or stabilization theorem. Thus “one template away from JC2” is not justified.
./xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:6551:2. **Resolved-pencil Noether/Cremona test of residue-A.**  
./xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:6566:   Test whether the formal \(42\)-conjugate Puiseux orbit satisfies any polynomial relation with the prescribed Newton support. First computation: at depth \(N=\#(\Delta_f\cap\mathbb Z^2)+20\), compute the monomial-evaluation matrix modulo two good primes on the saturated residue-A locus. Uniform full rank kills algebraization; a one-dimensional kernel reconstructs \(f\), after which \(J(f,g)=1\) is linear in the coefficients of \(g\).
./xmodel/codex-eval-20260812.md:6579:The program’s likeliest genuine breakthrough is a **global algebraization obstruction** for the residue-A template—effectivity, polar data, or coefficient transport at the resonant direction—not another vertex-local identity. The template has survived too many exact local, coefficient, and topological checks to expect an easy kill.
