9:# Proposition 5.8: the pole-mass identity on every fiber
10:
11:## Verdict
12:
13:Let
14:
15:\[
16:\mathcal F=(f,g):\mathbf A^2_{\mathbf C}\longrightarrow \mathbf A^2_{\mathbf C},
17:\qquad J(f,g)\in\mathbf C^*,
18:\]
19:
20:and put \(d=\operatorname{td}(f,g)=[\mathbf C(x,y):\mathbf C(f,g)]\).
21:With Sigray's notation (and the corrections already recorded in
22:`SIGRAY-AUDIT.md`), the assertion is
23:
24:\[
25:\boxed{
26:d=\sum_{F\in T_{a,\mathrm{pole}}}\Lambda(F)
27: =\sum_{F\in T_{a,\mathrm{pole}}}
28:   \frac{D_{g,F}\deg p_F}{\nu_F}
29:\quad\text{for every }a\in\mathbf C . }
30:\tag{P5.8}
31:\]
32:
33:This assertion is true. The relative pole-divisor argument has two distinct
34:parts:
35:
36:1. Its horizontal part is finite flat (in fact over all of
37:   \(\mathbf P^1_f\)) and has rank \(d\). On a nonempty open set of the
38:   \(f\)-line, its local intersection lengths are exactly Sigray's
39:   \(\Lambda(F)\)'s.
40:2. Flatness alone does **not** imply the result on a special fiber. There is
41:   an exact specialization defect, supported on boundary components on
42:   which \(f\) has a finite constant value and \(g\) is nonconstant. For a
43:   Keller map those components are excluded by Nguyen Van Chau's theorem on
44:   exceptional value curves. Hence the defect is zero for every \(a\).
45:
