===== ZHEGLOV-LTEST.md =====
1:# ZHEGLOV-LTEST: Falsification test of Lemma "L:polynomials" (arXiv:2410.06959 v5)
2:
3:Status: COMPLETE 2026-08-04. NOT REFUTED — 640/640 instances pass (see Verdict).
4:
5:## 1. Lemma statement (exact + plain terms)
6:Source: DC5.tex lines 1721-1731 (label `L:polynomials`). Exact statement:
7:
8:> Suppose g̃ ∈ ℂ[x] is a polynomial with more than one root. Suppose deg_x(g̃)=l̃,
9:> and d̃, z̃, A ∈ ℕ satisfy z̃=(A−1)d̃. Then there exists a solution H ∈ ℂ[x] of
10:>   c̃·g̃^A = H′·g̃ − ((z̃+1)/d̃)·H·g̃′,  c̃ ∈ ℂ*        (E:difeq*0)
11:> only if l̃/d̃ ∈ ℕ and l̃/d̃ > 1. In this case deg_x(H) = l̃(z̃+1)/d̃.
12:
13:Plain terms. Fix a complex polynomial g̃ of degree l̃ with **at least 2 distinct
14:roots** ("one root" means g=(1+αx)^l per line 1804). Fix integers A,d̃ ≥ 1 with
15:z̃=(A−1)d̃ ∈ ℕ; DC5.tex:377 defines ℕ = positive integers, so z̃≥1 forces **A≥2**.
16:Let λ=(z̃+1)/d̃ = A−1+1/d̃. Claims:
17:- (N) If d̃∤l̃, or l̃=d̃, then NO pair (H∈ℂ[x], c̃≠0) satisfies E:difeq*0.
18:- (D) If a solution exists (hence d̃|l̃, l̃/d̃≥2), then deg H = λl̃ = l̃(A−1)+l̃/d̃.
19:Note: the lemma does NOT claim existence; (N) is its load-bearing content.
20:Used at lines 2041 (kills case via l/d<1), 2198, 2301 (forces deg_y(H) and d/l=d_2)
21:— the S4 case-exhaustion engine.
22:
23:## 2. Hypothesis enforcement in random tests
24:- "more than one root": g̃ built as c·∏(x−r_i)^{m_i} with ≥2 *distinct* rationals
25:  r_i (sampled without replacement, asserted pairwise distinct), Σm_i = l̃.
26:- deg=l̃: by construction; leading coeff c random nonzero rational, asserted ≠0.
27:- d̃,z̃,A ∈ ℕ={1,2,...}: A≥2, d̃≥1 chosen; z̃:=(A−1)d̃≥1 computed, asserted ≥1.
28:  (A=1 ⇒ z̃=0 ∉ ℕ under the paper's convention: excluded entirely.)
29:- c̃∈ℂ*: equation is linear in (H,c̃); a solution with some c̃≠0 exists iff one
30:  with c̃=1 exists (scale H by 1/c̃). We solve T(H)=g̃^A, T(H):=H′g̃−λHg̃′.
31:- ℂ vs ℚ data: T is a ℚ-linear map once g̃∈ℚ[x]; solvability of a ℚ-coefficient
32:  linear system is field-independent (rank), so exact ℚ Gaussian elimination
33:  decides ℂ-solvability for that g̃. Degree-of-solution functionals (leading
34:  coeffs) are also ℚ-linear, so achievable degrees over ℂ = over ℚ.
35:- Completeness in deg H: if deg H=h≠λl̃, lead coeff of T(H) is (h−λl̃)·lc(H)·lc(g̃)≠0
36:  so deg T(H)=h+l̃−1, forcing h=(A−1)l̃+1; else h=λl̃ (needs d̃|l̃). So any solution
37:  has h ≤ Dmax=max((A−1)l̃+1, ⌊λl̃⌋); we solve with unknown deg ≤ Dmax+2.
38:- Kernel: T(H)=0 ⇒ H=c·g̃^λ, polynomial only if d̃=1; solver computes kernel
39:  dimension and the full set of achievable solution degrees, not just one solution.
40:
41:## 3. Test design
42:Script: `tests/ltest_polynomials.py` (stdlib only, Fraction arithmetic).
43:Per instance: build g̃, A, d̃; assemble (Dmax+3)×(Dmax+l̃) exact linear system
44:T(H)=g̃^A; Gauss-eliminate over ℚ; record solvable?/kernel dim/solution degrees.
