11:---
12:
13:## 1. The find
14:
15:### 1.1 What Żołądek proves
16:
17:Żołądek 2008 (the paper is **already in this repo at refs/zoladek2008_official.pdf**; SCOPE.md referee-risk
18:item 1 is answered out of the campaign's own reference folder), Appendix "Schwarz–Christoffel integrals",
19:studies Ψ(u) = ∫^u ∏_{i=1}^{s+1}(v−u_i)^{ϑ_i} dv (distinct u_i, rational exponents, s ≥ 1, ϑ_i ∈ Q∖Z_+)
20:and asks when Ψ is a **Darboux function** (const·∏(u−u_i)^{β_i}) modulo an additive constant. Verbatim
21:chain (pages 466–468):
22:
23:- **Lemma A.4.** Ψ Darboux ⟺ (i) at every ϑ_i ∈ Z_−∖0 the residue Res_{u_i}Φ = 0, and (ii) all
24:  "values" Ψ(u_i) coincide. [The residue-vanishing obstruction — the same functional as Corollary E.]
25:- **Lemma A.6.** If Ψ is Darboux then s + (1 + deg Φ) ∈ Z_−, deg R = −s − deg Φ; introduced with the
26:  sentence *"The following results were probably known already to Liouville."* Its proof is the
27:  valuation-at-infinity budget count (Ψ ~ u^{1+d} at ∞ vs. deg R + s + 1 + d) — the SAME mechanism as
28:  Theorem A's Steps 1–4 / the ord_∞ reading in MATHIEU.md §5.1.
29:- **Lemma A.7.** *"In the assumptions of Lemma A.6 one has that at least one ϑ_i ∉ Z."* I.e.: **if all
30:  exponents are negative integers and there are ≥ 2 distinct roots (s ≥ 1), the primitive is never a
31:  Darboux function.** Proof: normalize Ψ(∞) = 0, then Ψ = R·∏(u−u_i)^{ϑ_i+1} forces deg R = −s < 0,
32:  contradiction. (Four lines; identical shape to Theorem A's Step 4.)
33:
34:Over C every rational function is a Darboux function (split the numerator into linear factors), and A.4–A.7
35:work "modulo an additive constant"; so A.7 says: **∫ dv/A(v)^m is never a rational function when A has at
36:least two distinct roots, for any m ≥ 1** (exponents −m·e_i ∈ Z_−).
37:
38:Żołądek also uses this *inside the Jacobian problem in exactly Theorem A's role*: his Lemma 3.9 derives, from
39:Jac = 1 in a Newton–Puiseux chart, the linear inhomogeneous ODE (3.14) (ψ̃_N)′ = δ(ϕ′/ϕ)ψ̃_N − 1/(pϕ) —
40:i.e. **ϕ·ψ′ − δ·ϕ′·ψ = −1/p, our (ODE) with (generally rational) weight δ** — solves it as
41:ψ̃_N = −(1/p)ϕ^δ ∫ϕ^{−δ−1}, and imposes rationality of the mate ⟹ the Schwarz–Christoffel integral must be
42:Darboux. At §6 (p. 462, integer-exponent case): *"If s_{N−1} = 1, then we have an integral of a rational
43:function and by Lemma A.7 (in the Appendix) c_{N−1} cannot be Darbouxian."* — the integer-weight kill,
44:stated and used.
